 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Women's unemployment in Victoria has risen over the past year and is now about the same as men's. Employment is still high in absolute terms, but the employment-to-population ratio has slipped.\n\n**Women in Victoria (seasonally adjusted, latest is September 2026):**\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.1%, up from a low of 3.9% in April 2025. It peaked at 5.2% in July 2026 and has eased slightly since. It was 3.8% in January 2024.\n- **Unemployed:** about 99,000, compared with roughly 75,000–80,000 for most of 2025.\n- **Employed:** about 1.84 million. This is roughly flat over the past year (1.84 million in September 2025) but up about 89,000 since January 2024.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio:** 60.4%, down from about 61.3% in late 2025.\n- **Participation rate:** 63.6%, up from 62.9% in January 2024 but below the late-2025 level of about 63.9%.\n\n**Comparison with men (September 2026):**\n- **Unemployment rate:** 5.0%, so the gap has nearly closed. Men's rate has been higher than women's for most of the past few months.\n- **Participation rate:** 71.5%, still about 8 percentage points above women's.\n\nMonthly figures move around a fair bit, so the trend matters more than any single month. The main signal is the rise in unemployment since early 2026, with the sharpest increase between March and July.",
